e\nL484: \nL485: following simple example demonstrates.\nL486: \nL487: EXAMPLE 1.2.\nL488: \nL489: Let ti = sp(s.s’i on 10. I j or on 10, 1 J. The function\nL490: \nL491: f’(x) = 1 is adjoined to U since no nonzero element of sp{ 1. .v. .Y’ / has more\nL492: than two zeros, however, U is clearly not a T-space on 10. I 1 or on 10. I 1.\nL493: \nL494: A well-known property of WT-spaces that distinguishes them from T\nL495: \nL496: spaces is that of “degeneracy.”\nL497: \nL498: DEFINITION\nL499: \nL500: 2.2. lJ is said to be\nL501: \nL502: degenerate\nL503: \nL504: on an interval I if there\nL505: \nL506: exists a nontrivial element of U that vanishes identically on I. If U contains\nL507: \nL508: such an element we simply say that U is degenerate; otherwise U is called\nL509: \nL510: nondegenerare.\nL511: \nL512: T-spaces and linear spaces spanned by analytic functions, such as\nL513: polynomials, are examples of nondegenerate linear spaces.\nL514: \nL515: We now return to our original question. For the open interval an answer\nL516: \nL517: was provided by Stockenberg [ 6) and by Zalik 17 1; namely, a WT-space I/ is\nL518: \nL519: 104 D. ZWICK\nL520: \nL521: a T-space precisely when U is nondegenerate and contains a positive\nL522: \nL523: function (see also [4]). The situation is more complicated for the closed and\nL524: \nL525: the half-open interval.\nL526: \nL527: DEFINITION 3.2. A real-valued function f is said to have k separated\nL528: zeros if there exist points X, < y, < x2 < a.. < yk- 1 < xk such that f(xi) = 0\nL529: \nL530: (i = l,..., k) andf( yi) # 0 (i = l,..., k - 1).\nL531: \nL532: THEOREM 2.2 [6]. Let U be an n-dimensional WT-space with a positive\nL533: \nL534: function. If n is even then no element has more than n - 1 separated zeros. If\nL535: \nL536: n is odd then no element has more than n separated zeros and any element\nL537: \nL538: with n seperated zeros vanishes to the left of the first and to the right of the\nL539: \nL540: last zero.\nL541: Corollary 3.2 is easily deduced from Theorem 2.2, and provides an answer\nL542: \nL543: to our query in all but one instance.\nL544: \nL545: COROLLARY 3.2. Let U be an n-dimensional, non-degenerate WT-space\nL546: \nL547: on [a, b] with a positive function.\nL548: \nL549: (a) If n is even then U is a T-space on [a, b].\nL550: \nL551: (b) Zf n is odd then U is a T-space on (a, b), on (a, b], and on [a, b),\nL552: \nL553: but not necessarily on [a, b].\nL554: \nL555: By way of illustration, we present a counterexample of Niirnberger and\nL556: \nL557: Sommer 141.\nL558: EXAMPLE 2.2. Let U = sp( 1,x(1 -x*),x*}. U is a 3-dimensional,\nL559: \nL560: nondegenerate WT-space on [- 1, I] with a positive function. Although U is\nL561: \nL562: a T-space on 1-1, 1) and on (-1, 11, U is clearly not a T-space on [-1, 11.\nL563: \nL564: Our next goal is, therefore, to determine additional conditions on U that\nL565: \nL566: will make it a T-space on [a, b] regardless of the parity of n. Noting that a\nL567: \nL568: positive function generates a l-dimensional T-space, it is natural to\nL569: hypothesize that U contains a T-subspace of dimension greater than 1.\nL570: \nL571: DEFINITION 4.2. A WT-space is said to have T-rank k if it contains a T-\nL572: \nL573: space of dimension k but no T-space of dimension k + 1. T-rank 0 implies\nL574: \nL575: no T-subspaces.\nL576: \nL577: EXAMPLE 3.2. The space S,,,(< I,..., &J of splines of degree n - 1 with\nL578: \nL579: simple knots at 6, ,..., & (see [ 1, p. 181) is a WT-space of dimension n + k\nL580: \nL581: with T-rank n, as it contains the T-space of polynomials of degree < n - 1.\nL582: \nL583: DEGENERACY IN WT-SPACES\nL584: \nL585: 105\nL586: \nL587: THEOREM\nL588: 3.2. Let U be a nondegenerate WT-space of dimension n on\nL589: \nL590: 1 a, b 1. If U has T-rank > 2 then U is a T-space on /a. b 1.\nL591: \nL592: Proof:\nL593: \nL594: Suppose that u E U is nontrivial and has II zeros.\nL595: \nL596: a < X, < .. . < x,, <\nL597: \